==Old English==

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|gem-pro|ang}} {{recons|þurbaną|lang=gem-pro}}, from {{etyl|ine-pro|ang}} {{recons|terp-|lang=ine-pro}}.  Germanic cognates include Old Frisian {{recons|thurva|lang=ofs}}}}, Old Saxon {{term|thurvan|lang=osx}}, Middle Dutch {{term|durven|lang=dum}} (Dutch {{term|durven|lang=nl}}), Old Norse {{term|þurfa|lang=non}} (Swedish {{term|tarva|lang=sv}}), Old High German {{Term|thurfan|lang=goh}} (German {{term|dürfen|lang=de}}), Gothic {{term|𐌸𐌰𐌿𐍂𐌱𐌰𐌽|lang=got}}.  The Indo-European root also led to Ancient Greek {{term|τερπειν|lang=grc}}, Old Church Slavonic {{term|tr=trěbě|lang=cu}}, Russian {{term|требоватъ|lang=ru}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/ˈðurvɑn/|lang=ang}}

===Verb===
{{ang-verb|type=pretpres}}

# to [[need]], to be [[obliged]] (to do something)

